it's the western entrance into the  ancient city of oya it means new door  but it dates back thousands of years  oya was established by the phoenicians  nearly 3 000 years ago it then came  under roman control and flourished in  the second and third centuries a.d  it was conquered by the rashidun khalifa  and was renamed tripoli in the 7th  century  it's one of five unesco world heritage  sites in libya that are on its  endangered list  while it's been neglected in recent  decades that's starting to change  local authorities with the help of  residents have begun a renovation  campaign to save the old city  the old city is a symbol for all of  libya the city hasn't seen renovations  since the 1940s all the governments that  libya has seen since then neglected the  city it was on the verge of collapse but  thanks to the association and the  residents we're seeing new life put into  the heart of tripoli  the bab jadeed entrance has just  reopened to the public with a new look  and shop owners like ali mabrouk  couldn't be happier  before there weren't many customers but  after the renovations the market changed  new customers are coming in people are  visiting because they want to see the  new look  hadia  is on the board of directors of the old  city association and has been  supervising the renovation work she says  it's been her dream to breathe new life  into tripoli's old city yes i'm super  proud of course i mean it's something  uh  you know it's a dream coming true and  not even my dream my father used to work  in the in this organization my father  died i want to tell him you know i hope  he's looking you know saying okay see  you we've done what uh what you always  hoped so personally for me it's a it's a  yeah it's a big  it's a big thing i'm very proud you can  feel how the entire vial of the old city  has changed with these new renovations  and locals now have a sense of pride in  the city's rich history and a renewed  sense of hope that it could return to  its former glory naima used to live in  the old city she found out about the  renovations on social media  i brought my grandchildren to see their  history this change is amazing i hope  they run away the entire city 40 years  ago when i lived here the roads and  homes were falling apart this is a great  beginning libya has seen relative calm  since a nationwide ceasefire was signed  last october people here hope the  hard-won piece won't be short-lived so  the work can continue to maintain  libya's historical sites malak trina al  jazeera tripoli
 

